WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.914
Click Jupiter.

00:00:00.914 --> 00:00:06.787
Find the Great Red Spot — the oval, roughly the size of Earth, in the southern hemisphere.

00:00:06.787 --> 00:00:09.724
Now stop and consider what you're looking at.

00:00:09.724 --> 00:00:11.681
That is a single storm system.

00:00:11.681 --> 00:00:16.641
It has been there, continuously, for at least three hundred and fifty years.

00:00:16.641 --> 00:00:20.165
Astronomers first noted it through telescopes in 1665.

00:00:20.165 --> 00:00:21.470
It was a storm then.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:22.645
It is a storm now.

00:00:22.645 --> 00:00:35.697
Whatever combination of fluid dynamics, atmospheric chemistry, and Jupiter's rapid rotation sustains it has been keeping it spinning continuously since before the discovery of Newton's laws of motion.

00:00:35.697 --> 00:00:39.677
Jupiter rotates once every nine hours and fifty-five minutes.

00:00:39.677 --> 00:00:42.875
Faster than any other planet in the Solar System.

00:00:42.875 --> 00:00:50.771
That rotation drives winds in the Great Red Spot that reach four hundred and thirty kilometers per hour at its outer rim.

00:00:50.771 --> 00:00:53.186
Triple the strongest Earth hurricane.

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:55.013
The spot has been shrinking.

00:00:55.013 --> 00:00:58.146
In 1879 it was forty thousand kilometers across.

00:00:58.146 --> 00:01:00.495
Today it is around fifteen thousand.

00:01:00.495 --> 00:01:11.589
Whether it eventually closes — or whether something keeps reseeding it out of the bands of upwelling gas around it — is one of the open questions Juno was sent to answer.

00:01:11.589 --> 00:01:12.959
Juno arrived in 2016.

00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:26.925
Its orbit takes it over Jupiter's poles — a polar orbit, a frame no previous Jupiter mission used — so it can map the planet's magnetic field, atmospheric chemistry, and the interior structure below the cloud tops.

00:01:26.925 --> 00:01:33.777
Photographs of the Great Red Spot from Juno are some of the most detailed ever taken from any spacecraft.

00:01:33.777 --> 00:01:36.518
Earth's worst hurricane lasts a few weeks.

00:01:36.518 --> 00:01:40.825
Jupiter has had the same hurricane for three and a half centuries.

00:01:40.825 --> 00:01:41.543
Look at it.

00:01:41.543 --> 00:01:43.240
Then notice the scale bar.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:44.088
Then breathe.

